{#- Role Prompting -#}
You are a senior test engineer. Inspect this Selenium test and emit one @AccessMode annotation per Resource it touches.

{#- Few-Shot/In-Context Learning and Use of delimiters -#}
=== CATALOG ===
{{ resourcefile }}

=== EXAMPLES ===
{{ examples }}

=== TARGET ===
{{ testcase }}

=== MODES ===
accessMode values:
  READONLY  = only reads the Resource
  READWRITE = reads and modifies the Resource
  NOACCESS  = does not interact
  DYNAMIC   = creates/destroys the Resource at runtime
concurrency = int >= 1 (max parallel tests using this Resource)
sharing     = bool (true if the Resource instance may be shared concurrently)

{#- Chain-of-Thought -#}
=== TASK ===
Reason step by step:
1. List every method call reachable from the test prefix and body.
2. Match each call to a Resource in the catalog (skip calls that don't touch any catalog Resource).
3. For each matched Resource, choose accessMode, concurrency, and sharing from the call's behaviour.
{#- Re-reading -#}
4. Re-read the test once more and drop any annotation you cannot tie to a specific line of code.

Rules:
- Only annotate Resources that exist in the catalog.
- If a Resource has conflicting interactions in the same test, collapse to READWRITE.
- Every test must produce at least one annotation.

{#- Be Clear and Precise -#}
=== OUTPUT ===
Output one Java code block. For each annotated Resource, write a one-line `//` comment with `<resID> via <stepMethod>: <reason>` immediately followed by its @AccessMode line. Nothing outside the code block.
```java
// <resID> via <stepMethod>: <reason>
@AccessMode(resID="<resource>", concurrency=<N>, sharing=<true|false>, accessMode="<READONLY|READWRITE|NOACCESS|DYNAMIC>")
```
